  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>
    Modern food is calorie-dense and nutrient-sparse by design. The Mitigate pillar
    approaches nutrition through the lens of sourcing and quality, not just macros.
    The same protein gram from a grass-finished ribeye and a feedlot-CAFO chicken breast
    carry meaningfully different inflammatory, hormonal, and micronutrient profiles.
  </p>
  <p>
    We don't prescribe a single dietary ideology. We prescribe a sourcing standard:
    animals raised on their natural diet, minimal processing, no seed oils, real food first.
    The My4MLife App includes weekly nutrition anchors aligned to the 4M protocol.
  </p>
  <p>Inflammatory food sourcing is a MindSpan tax paid at every meal — the sourcing standard below is one of the simplest levers for moving your score in the right direction.</p>


  <aside class="protein-first-rule">
    <span class="protein-first-tag">Universal Rule</span>
    <h2>The 30g Protein-First Protocol</h2>
    <p><strong>Every feeding window opens with 30g+ lean protein, 30 minutes before any other food.</strong> Whether you eat once a day or six times, the first 30 minutes of any feeding window is protein only. Then the rest of the meal follows.</p>
    <p>The 30g threshold isn't arbitrary — it's the leucine-signal threshold that triggers maximal muscle protein synthesis in adult men, particularly after 40 when sarcopenia begins. The 30-minute spacing lets that anabolic signal land cleanly before glucose, fats, and competing macros arrive. On GLP-1 protocols where total intake drops sharply, this rule is even more critical — it protects the lean mass that drives metabolic flexibility and visceral-fat reduction.</p>
    <p class="protein-first-examples"><em>What 30g of lean protein looks like:</em> 5 oz chicken or turkey breast · 4–5 oz lean grass-finished beef · 5 oz wild salmon · 5–6 eggs · 1.5 cups high-protein Greek yogurt · 1 scoop whey isolate with cottage cheese · 5 oz wild tuna.</p>
  </aside>

